#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147226 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2175" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{-686}{2} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =-343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =-343 \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(-7)^{3} \\\\ r & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{20}{80} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2179" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{9} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{9-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192,$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=6 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{8}=192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{8}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{192}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{8}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{192}{6} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{8}}{{r}^{3}} & =32 \\\\ {r}^{5} & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio satisfies $r^5 = 32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-250943" :question/problem "Find the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose tenth term is $-8$ and whose twelfth term is $-72.$ You're given that the common ratio is negative.\n\n$r=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{12}=-72$ and ${a}_{10}=-8.$ Substituting $n=12$ and $m=10$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{12-10} & =\\frac{{a}_{12}}{{a}_{10}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{(-72)}{(-8)} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is negative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(-3)^{2} \\\\ r & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554067} {:answer/id #uuid "82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554132} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554207} {:answer/id #uuid "d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554323} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554323} :answer-field/id #uuid "7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-63999" :question/problem "The second term of a geometric sequence is $3$ and its fourth term is $27.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{2}=3.$ Substituting $n=4$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{27}{3} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4748909d-6528-5bed-ac92-aa61a9379c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554004} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1289eb-910a-5785-b6b0-3fd7998b0a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554018} {:answer/id #uuid "5685f6f8-da97-57f5-82fe-f48a3267d960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554037} {:answer/id #uuid "a499d257-2f0a-5c4a-b368-feeb1f43f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554230} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d91c8e-478d-51eb-a6d6-2618550a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a499d257-2f0a-5c4a-b368-feeb1f43f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554230} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e4ad903-4e09-5e2b-9085-556ef9945de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7794f339-0fd4-50d9-a0ab-1ce4d82318cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64032" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose second term is $48$ and whose fifth term is $6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=6$ and ${a}_{2}=48.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{6}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553913} {:answer/id #uuid "a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554235} {:answer/id #uuid "cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554318} {:answer/id #uuid "f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554397} {:answer/id #uuid "fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554409} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798" :question/math-academy-id "q-64033" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $135$ and whose seventh term is $5.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula \n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{7}=5$ and ${a}_{4}=135.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{5}{135} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d948b64-117c-579e-b644-ff48d5689b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553899} {:answer/id #uuid "414e8dd7-f46d-5b69-b647-2ab2091b8a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553987} {:answer/id #uuid "66e2c5cc-c66f-5b95-bdf4-64edd8104ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b8e80e-0266-5cc0-9779-10e7d69bf61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554231} {:answer/id #uuid "c88fe9b9-712f-5caa-8196-19c17631b170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=2\\,187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d948b64-117c-579e-b644-ff48d5689b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553899} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf33f80-cdc5-56ca-aedb-5cb060109b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3805f218-eb22-5e05-bae4-085089844c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-67512" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $27$ and whose $11$th term is $-\\frac{1}{81}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=11$ and $n=4,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{11} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{11-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{11}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=27 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{10}=-\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{10}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{10}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{10}}{{r}^{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187} \\\\ {r}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f06b55-7101-5453-94d2-1449f30ba91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}=\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553884} {:answer/id #uuid "4384dfb2-d047-515d-a013-fca808c9af4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553993} {:answer/id #uuid "75296d26-4773-53b7-af61-4667830eee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554106} {:answer/id #uuid "c313cfcb-f47c-5df3-a1fb-6a7052dd4a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554293} {:answer/id #uuid "f76e274a-b078-575d-9e2e-8d6701796c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c313cfcb-f47c-5df3-a1fb-6a7052dd4a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554293} :answer-field/id #uuid "01cea72a-2cdc-51be-a48d-be98f9d7fefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8e2a0b2-6f85-51c0-a653-f0669492c9a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67513" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose $3$rd term is $4$ and whose $5$th term is $196.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=5$ and $n=3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{3-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{2}, \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{5-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{3}=4$ and ${a}_{5}=196,$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{2}=4 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{4}=196\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{4}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{2}} & =\\frac{196}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{4}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{2}} & =\\frac{196}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{4}}{{r}^{2}} & =49 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
